Yes — if you mean clowning as the L.A. dance movement, that is almost a perfect modern example of what we were describing.

Tommy the Clown developed the style in the early 1990s around his hip-hop clown performances. Kids copied it, crews formed, competitions emerged, and variants became more extreme. Out of that ecology came krumping, which developed into its own recognizable movement vocabulary. 

That gives us a cleaner pattern than the marathon example because we can watch the cultural evolution happen almost in real time:

one person exaggerates/combines existing movements → signature style appears → people imitate → imitation creates crews → crews compete → competition selects interesting mutations → mutations split into new styles → the movement becomes bigger than its inventor

And there is another ingredient: permission to mutate. Tommy did not require everybody to reproduce one exact choreography. The recognizable grammar survived while individuals added their own signatures. That is why you get an evolutionary tree instead of merely a copied routine.

So for creating something novel that propagates, you probably want to design less like a single “move” and more like a movement grammar:

canonical move + recognizable constraints + open mutation space + social demonstration + challenge/battle/ritual + lineage.

Then someone else can invent something you never thought of inside the space you created. That is when you know the thing has become generative rather than merely imitative.